#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125528} :activity/id #uuid "76681192-0bc8-5fd9-98fc-5fd254bc93fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049255 :topic/id #uuid "e711f285-9c99-50d8-8dee-a4a8b01a7c1a" :topic/math-academy-id 1936} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360547}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "2f62c8fc-daae-562a-a01e-851a375ec7a4" :step/math-academy-id 12678 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125749 :tutorial/content "We can consider $f(\\mathbf{r}(t))$ as the values taken by the function $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$ So, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))]\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the **rate of change of $f$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$**\n\nFurthermore, if $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is a level curve $f(x,y)=c,$ then by the chain rule we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =c \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(f(r(t))) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(c) \\\\ ∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can conclude that the gradient vector $\\nabla f$ is orthogonal to every level curve of $f.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "06350cc4-9c76-53db-a464-43e93f2fcc2f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2652 :tutorial/title "The Derivative as a Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "ea045ecb-90e1-5dc8-804d-711207e5f86c" :step/math-academy-id 12681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124277 :question/id #uuid "033e459e-5148-5e78-ab5c-7e455b06cd64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3695"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360579}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"} :step/id #uuid "ef0f7fd7-782e-55cb-8008-f28d770450ed" :step/math-academy-id 12682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124278 :question/id #uuid "f44b6ea6-694c-5f54-94ee-4c4a699bf4eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3696"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360529}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "cde1eff3-797d-5f65-a385-6296a6f32db3" :step/math-academy-id 12684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125835 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the function \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{r}) = f(x,y) = xy^2,\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t) \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nas input. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right]?$\n\nTo do this, we use the **multivariable chain rule in vector form**, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now apply this to our functions. First, we compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(f(x,y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(f(x,y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{2},2xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2},2(\\operatorname{cos}t)(\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩ \\\\  & =-{sin}^{3}t+2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "673ff670-99f1-5416-aa83-5c4c8bf0e4f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4967 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "87b55278-4bb6-5686-b4ef-178d5036b236" :step/math-academy-id 30935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124404 :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360525}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "4850b0cc-54ab-5a26-9440-b0d1cf757680" :step/math-academy-id 30936 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125836 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for a function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x_1(t), x_2(t), \\ldots, x_m(t) \\right\\rangle$ is a vector-valued function of $m$ variables, is the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nHow does this chain rule in vector form relate to the chain rule we have seen before, which states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d f} {\\textrm d t} \t= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_1}{\\textrm d t}  \n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_2}{\\textrm d t} + \\dots + \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_m}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet's expand $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)$ to see. Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))=⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{\\text{d}t}+⋯+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey are exactly the same, just different notations!\n\n**Note**: If we replace $\\mathbf{r}$ with a single-valued function $g(t),$ we get back the chain rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(g(t))] & =∇f(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "15062cce-eede-545a-ae5d-e02432a0ba33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4968 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Vector Form of the Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "fd19c878-fabf-5b99-9507-9a32fb862324" :step/math-academy-id 30937}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124728}]}
